    "result_text": "The build succeeded and the test suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the `sharesHousehold` / `householdId` feature on `POST /api/owners`, following the OfficeFloor function-injection pattern the project uses.\n\nThe `sharesHousehold` request flag and the 409-on-duplicate-household behavior already existed (via `EnsureHouseholdUnique`). What was missing was assigning and returning a shared `householdId`. I added:\n\n1. **`householdId` on the `Owner` schema** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 a `readOnly` field so it's returned from the API and generated onto `OwnerDto`.\n\n2. **`householdId` on the `Owner` entity** (`Owner.java`) \u2014 new `household_id` column with getter/setter. MapStruct auto-maps it by name.\n\n3. **DB column `household_id`** added to all four schemas (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n4. **New step `AssignHouseholdId`** (`function/owner/AssignHouseholdId.java`) \u2014 runs after the uniqueness checks pass. When the new owner shares a household (same lastName + address, normalized the same way as `EnsureHouseholdUnique`) with one or more existing owners, it assigns them all a **stable, deterministic** `householdId` (`H-` + first 12 upper-case hex of `SHA-256(lastName|address)`), back-filling existing members so both sides carry the shared id. A lone owner keeps a null `householdId`.\n\n5. **Wired the step** into `owners.POST.yml` between `assignCode` and `save`, under `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\nBecause the id is derived deterministically from the normalized lastName+address, every member of a household resolves to the same value regardless of creation order \u2014 satisfying the acceptance test's assertion that joiners b and c share a non-blank `householdId`.",
